// North Carolina: the third playable state, and the one that asks the most of the terrain.
//
// South Carolina rises to 1,085 m and Florida barely rises at all. This state runs from the Outer Banks
// to Mount Mitchell -- 2,037 m, the highest ground east of the Mississippi -- in about 800 km, so the
// generator has to route interstates through a mountain range, bridge a sound, and still keep every
// grade drivable. The Appalachian end of I-40 and the Blue Ridge are the test.
//
// Places are real, as always; everything in them is invented. Start is Boone, up in the mountains, which
// is a different opening to Longs: no ocean, a hard climb out in any direction, and the nearest city an
// hour away.
{
  "name": "North Carolina",
  "abbreviation": "NC",
  "scale": 25.0,
  // Lower than the other two states, and it has to be. Heights are exaggerated so that South
  // Carolina's gentle upstate reads as hills at all; here the hills are real, and a 3x exaggeration
  // turns a genuine 5% mountain interstate into a 15% wall that nothing can climb. At 1.5x Mount
  // Mitchell still stands 122 m over the map and the roads into Boone and Asheville are drivable.
  "verticalScale": 1.5,
  "origin": [-81.674, 36.217],

